    expectedAnswer: "Clean Architecture organizes code so business rules are less dependent on frameworks, UI, databases, or transport details. Dependencies should point toward stable inner policies, while outer adapters implement the interfaces required by those policies.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Define the use-case contract independently from React or HTTP.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface GetUser {\n  execute(id: string): Promise<string>;\n}\n\ninterface UserSource {\n  find(id: string): Promise<string | null>;\n}\n\nfunction createGetUser(source: UserSource): GetUser {\n  return {\n    async execute(id) {\n      const name = await source.find(id);\n\n      if (name === null) {\n        throw new Error(\"User not found\");\n      }\n\n      return name;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — An adapter can implement the port.\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / fetch example:\n```ts\ninterface UserSource {\n  find(id: string): Promise<string | null>;\n}\n\nfunction createGetUser(source: UserSource) {\n  return {\n    async execute(id: string): Promise<string> {\n      const name = await source.find(id);\n      if (name === null) throw new Error(\"User not found\");\n      return name;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst source: UserSource = {\n  async find(id) {\n    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n\n    if (!response.ok) return null;\n\n    const data = (await response.json()) as { name?: string };\n    return data.name ?? null;\n  },\n};\n\nconst getUser = createGetUser(source);\n```\n\nStep 3 — The core use case does not need to know whether the source is fetch, IndexedDB, a mock, or another service.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nThe central idea is dependency direction, not a fixed folder-count template.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
