    title: "Hexagonal Architecture",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Software Architecture Fundamentals",
    expectedAnswer: "Hexagonal Architecture, or Ports and Adapters, keeps the core application independent from external technologies. Ports are the interfaces the core needs or exposes; adapters connect those ports to HTTP, storage, messaging, browsers, or other external systems.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Define an output port and use case.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface NotificationPort {\n  send(message: string): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nasync function publishWelcome(\n  email: string,\n  notifier: NotificationPort,\n): Promise<void> {\n  await notifier.send(`Welcome ${email}`);\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Create an adapter.\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / browser adapter:\n```ts\ninterface NotificationPort {\n  send(message: string): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nasync function publishWelcome(\n  email: string,\n  notifier: NotificationPort,\n): Promise<void> {\n  await notifier.send(`Welcome ${email}`);\n}\n\nconst browserNotification: NotificationPort = {\n  async send(message) {\n    console.log(\"Notification:\", message);\n  },\n};\n\npublishWelcome(\"user@example.com\", browserNotification).then(() => {\n  console.log(\"Welcome notification sent\");\n});\n```\n\nStep 3 — Swap the adapter without changing the use case.\n\nAnother adapter could call a REST API, queue, or test double.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nThe core talks through ports; adapters translate external details to and from those ports.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
    productionExample: "A frontend application can expose feature-level ports for analytics, persistence, and notifications while individual adapters handle browser APIs, network clients, or test mocks.",
    bestPractices: ["Start from the business problem and change patterns.", "Define ownership and dependency direction explicitly.", "Prefer the simplest boundary that is useful today.", "Keep infrastructure details behind stable interfaces when substitution has real value.", "Document important architectural decisions and assumptions."],
    tradeOffs: "Ports make integration boundaries explicit and easy to replace, but too many ports for simple integrations can create unnecessary ceremony.",
