    expectedAnswer: "Feature-based Architecture organizes code around user capabilities or business features rather than technical types such as one global components folder, one hooks folder, and one services folder. The benefit is local ownership: UI, state, API calls, tests, and domain logic for a feature stay close together.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Model a feature contract.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface CartItem {\n  id: string;\n  quantity: number;\n}\n\ninterface CartState {\n  items: CartItem[];\n}\n\nfunction addToCart(\n  state: CartState,\n  id: string,\n): CartState {\n  const existing = state.items.find((item) => item.id === id);\n\n  if (existing) {\n    return {\n      items: state.items.map((item) =>\n        item.id === id\n          ? { ...item, quantity: item.quantity + 1 }\n          : item,\n      ),\n    };\n  }\n\n  return {\n    items: [...state.items, { id, quantity: 1 }],\n  };\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — A feature folder can own this state, UI, tests, and API adapter.\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / derived selector:\n```ts\ninterface CartItem {\n  id: string;\n  quantity: number;\n}\n\ninterface CartState {\n  items: CartItem[];\n}\n\nfunction cartCount(state: CartState): number {\n  return state.items.reduce(\n    (total, item) => total + item.quantity,\n    0,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nExample structure:\n`features/cart/`\n→ components\n→ state\n→ api\n→ model\n→ tests\n\nInterview takeaway:\nThe boundary follows a capability that users or teams recognize, not merely a file type.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
