    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Model a value object with a business invariant.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass EmailAddress {\n  readonly value: string;\n\n  constructor(value: string) {\n    const normalized = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n\n    if (!normalized.includes(\"@\")) {\n      throw new Error(\"Invalid email\");\n    }\n\n    this.value = normalized;\n  }\n\n  equals(other: EmailAddress): boolean {\n    return this.value === other.value;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Use domain language in the application code.\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / example:\n```ts\nclass EmailAddress {\n  readonly value: string;\n\n  constructor(value: string) {\n    const normalized = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n    if (!normalized.includes(\"@\")) throw new Error(\"Invalid email\");\n    this.value = normalized;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction registerCustomer(email: string): EmailAddress {\n  return new EmailAddress(email);\n}\n\nconst customerEmail = registerCustomer(\"User@Example.com\");\nconsole.log(customerEmail.value);\n```\n\nStep 3 — The domain object owns the invariant instead of allowing every UI layer to validate it differently.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nDDD is most valuable where the business rules are complex and the language itself is part of the design problem.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
