    title: "CQRS Basics",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Software Architecture Fundamentals",
    expectedAnswer: "CQRS separates command responsibilities from query responsibilities. A command changes state; a query reads state. The separation is useful when read and write workloads, models, authorization rules, or scaling needs differ materially.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Define separate command and query contracts.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface UpdateProfileCommand {\n  userId: string;\n  displayName: string;\n}\n\nfunction updateProfile(\n  state: Record<string, string>,\n  command: UpdateProfileCommand,\n): Record<string, string> {\n  return {\n    ...state,\n    [command.userId]: command.displayName,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getProfile(\n  state: Readonly<Record<string, string>>,\n  userId: string,\n): string | null {\n  return state[userId] ?? null;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — The command model and query model can evolve separately.\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / example:\n```ts\ninterface UpdateProfileCommand {\n  userId: string;\n  displayName: string;\n}\n\nfunction updateProfile(\n  state: Record<string, string>,\n  command: UpdateProfileCommand,\n): Record<string, string> {\n  return {\n    ...state,\n    [command.userId]: command.displayName,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getProfile(\n  state: Readonly<Record<string, string>>,\n  userId: string,\n): string | null {\n  return state[userId] ?? null;\n}\n\nconst state = updateProfile(\n  {},\n  { userId: \"1\", displayName: \"Rasik\" },\n);\n\nconst result = getProfile(state, \"1\");\n```\n\nInterview takeaway:\nCQRS is not the same as event sourcing. You can separate commands and queries without storing every change as an event.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
