    category: "Software Architecture Fundamentals",
    expectedAnswer: "Event-driven Architecture communicates changes through events rather than requiring every consumer to be directly called by the producer. Events represent something that happened, while consumers react asynchronously or synchronously according to the system design.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the architecture principle.\n\nStep 1 — Define a typed event and subscriber registry.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface UserCreated {\n  type: \"user.created\";\n  userId: string;\n}\n\ntype AppEvent = UserCreated;\n\nclass EventBus {\n  private readonly handlers = new Map<\n    AppEvent[\"type\"],\n    Array<(event: AppEvent) => void>\n  >();\n\n  on(\n    type: AppEvent[\"type\"],\n    handler: (event: AppEvent) => void,\n  ): void {\n    const handlers = this.handlers.get(type) ?? [];\n    handlers.push(handler);\n    this.handlers.set(type, handlers);\n  }\n\n  emit(event: AppEvent): void {\n    for (const handler of this.handlers.get(event.type) ?? []) {\n      handler(event);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / usage:\n```ts\ninterface UserCreated {\n  type: \"user.created\";\n  userId: string;\n}\n\ntype AppEvent = UserCreated;\n\nclass EventBus {\n  private readonly handlers = new Map<\n    AppEvent[\"type\"],\n    Array<(event: AppEvent) => void>\n  >();\n\n  on(\n    type: AppEvent[\"type\"],\n    handler: (event: AppEvent) => void,\n  ): void {\n    const handlers = this.handlers.get(type) ?? [];\n    handlers.push(handler);\n    this.handlers.set(type, handlers);\n  }\n\n  emit(event: AppEvent): void {\n    for (const handler of this.handlers.get(event.type) ?? []) {\n      handler(event);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconst bus = new EventBus();\n\nbus.on(\"user.created\", (event) => {\n  console.log(\"Analytics:\", event.userId);\n});\n\nbus.emit({\n  type: \"user.created\",\n  userId: \"1\",\n});\n```\n\nStep 3 — Consumers can subscribe without the producer knowing every consumer.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nEvents reduce direct coupling, but they introduce ordering, duplication, retry, and observability concerns.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what changes this boundary protects, what it costs, and why it is justified.",
